(Regression test for bug #47: `Seek ... to line N` kept its line counter in
rcx across the read syscall. The kernel clobbers rcx with the return address,
so the counter became a code address on the very first byte read, the scan
stopped at the first newline, and every target above line 2 landed at the
start of line 2 - and because the scan never ran on, it never reached EOF, so
a line past the end of the file never set the error flag either.

Two shapes, because they are not the same test. The first gives every seek a
fresh handle, which is how the ledger's D3 repro found this. The second walks
one handle out of order - 5, then back to 3, then past the end - which is what
proves the seek is absolute rather than a scan that happens to accumulate.)

open a file for writing called setup at "/tmp/vox_test_seek_line_positions.txt".
Write "AA\nBBBB\nCCCCCC\nDD\nEEEEEEEE\n" to setup.
Close setup.

(All five lines are different lengths, so the line we land on is unambiguous.)

(A fresh handle per seek.)
open a file for reading called 'the first reader' at "/tmp/vox_test_seek_line_positions.txt".
a buffer called 'the first line' is 32 bytes in size.
Seek 'the first reader' to line 1.
On error print "BUG: seek to line 1 fired the error handler", exit 1.
Read line from 'the first reader' into 'the first line'.
print "fresh handle, line 1 -> {'the first line'}".
Close 'the first reader'.

open a file for reading called 'the second reader' at "/tmp/vox_test_seek_line_positions.txt".
a buffer called 'the second line' is 32 bytes in size.
Seek 'the second reader' to line 2.
On error print "BUG: seek to line 2 fired the error handler", exit 1.
Read line from 'the second reader' into 'the second line'.
print "fresh handle, line 2 -> {'the second line'}".
Close 'the second reader'.

open a file for reading called 'the third reader' at "/tmp/vox_test_seek_line_positions.txt".
a buffer called 'the third line' is 32 bytes in size.
Seek 'the third reader' to line 3.
On error print "BUG: seek to line 3 fired the error handler", exit 1.
Read line from 'the third reader' into 'the third line'.
print "fresh handle, line 3 -> {'the third line'}".
Close 'the third reader'.

open a file for reading called 'the fifth reader' at "/tmp/vox_test_seek_line_positions.txt".
a buffer called 'the fifth line' is 32 bytes in size.
Seek 'the fifth reader' to line 5.
On error print "BUG: seek to line 5 fired the error handler", exit 1.
Read line from 'the fifth reader' into 'the fifth line'.
print "fresh handle, line 5 -> {'the fifth line'}".
Close 'the fifth reader'.

open a file for reading called 'the far reader' at "/tmp/vox_test_seek_line_positions.txt".
a buffer called 'the far line' is 32 bytes in size.
Seek 'the far reader' to line 99.
On error print "fresh handle, line 99 past the end set the error flag".
Read line from 'the far reader' into 'the far line'.
print "fresh handle, line 99 read {'the far line's size} bytes".
Close 'the far reader'.

(One handle, walked out of order: forward to 5, back to 3, then past the end.
A seek to a line is absolute, so going back must really go back.)
open a file for reading called 'the walking reader' at "/tmp/vox_test_seek_line_positions.txt".
a buffer called 'the walked line' is 32 bytes in size.

Seek 'the walking reader' to line 5.
On error print "BUG: walking seek to line 5 fired the error handler", exit 1.
Read line from 'the walking reader' into 'the walked line'.
print "one handle, line 5 -> {'the walked line'}".

Seek 'the walking reader' to line 3.
On error print "BUG: walking seek back to line 3 fired the error handler", exit 1.
Read line from 'the walking reader' into 'the walked line'.
print "one handle, back to line 3 -> {'the walked line'}".

Seek 'the walking reader' to line 99.
On error print "one handle, line 99 past the end set the error flag".

Close 'the walking reader'.
Delete the file "/tmp/vox_test_seek_line_positions.txt".
